Description: Let a close tag close back to a matching open ancestor
 A close tag that did not match the current element was dropped, so one
 element left open, such as Drupal's never-closed "<head-placeholder>",
 swallowed every later close tag and nested the rest of the document inside
 it. Following HTML's "any other end tag" step, a close tag that matches an
 open ancestor now leaves the elements in between unclosed and closes the
 ancestor. A close tag that matches nothing open is still dropped. The
 ancestor is found with one walk up the open elements, each element's name
 key is computed once, and every element in between is closed in the same
 step, so closing back over thousands of open elements stays linear.
Author: Numiko
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no
Test: test/parser/parse-for-eslint.test.ts ("Twig as part of the markup structure", "parses 20,000 elements left open inside each other, closed back by one close tag", "drops 20,000 stray close tags inside deep nesting"); upstream snapshot "Custom tag raw content" updated: </markdown> now closes <markdown>
Last-Update: 2026-10-19
